# Package Split

The package split migrates bounded parts of this repository into independently
released packages under datadog-apm-python. The authoritative docs are
`docs/package-split/plan.md` (plan and validation gates) and
`docs/package-split/agent-prompt.md` (execution guardrails); the tooling
reference is `scripts/package_split/README.md`. The `package-split` skill
(`.claude/skills/package-split/SKILL.md`) drives the full workflow.

## Key facts

- Destination repository: https://github.com/DataDog/datadog-apm-python/
  (private to Datadog) — a uv-workspace monorepo; packages live under
  `packages/<name>/` (src layout, shared ruff/pyright/deptry pins at the
  root). It already hosts the slim seed package `packages/ddtrace-internal`
  (published 0.1.0 provides only `_ddtrace_internal`). New import roots must
  be registered in `.importlinter` `root_packages` (enforced by
  `scripts/validate_packages.py`); API versioning uses
  `scripts/api_semver_check.py` + `release/<package>:<level>` labels.
  Working layer rule of thumb: `ddtrace_internal_base` = pure-Python,
  stdlib-only; `ddtrace_internal_native` = compiled code required for
  `ddtrace_internal`; `ddtrace_internal` = current `ddtrace.internal` code,
  using the above as dependencies. Acyclic: `ddtrace_internal` -> base +
  native; nothing depends on `ddtrace_internal`. Final names pending plan
  review.
- `package-contract.toml` (repo root) is the machine-readable package contract,
  validated against a versioned schema. The current one is PROVISIONAL and
  records the single-distribution reality; it tightens as extraction proceeds.
- `scripts/package-split <gate>` runs the versioned harness in
  `scripts/package_split/`: `boundaries`, `contracts`, `artifacts`,
  `lifecycle`, `compatibility`, `migration`, `freeze`, plus the informational
  `api-usage` / `api-surface` surface analyses. Gates are fail-closed: a
  missing prerequisite or unexecuted required scenario is `not_run` and never
  counts as passing. Every gate writes JSON evidence with per-check results.
- New-namespace packages must be import-inert (no threads, network, logging
  changes, import hooks, patched callables at import time), must not import
  `ddtrace`, and `native-free` packages must stay native-free across the whole
  resolved runtime graph.
- Compatibility bridges in ddtrace fall back only when the new top-level
  package is genuinely absent; a broken new installation must fail loudly.
- `package-freeze.toml` pins extraction provenance (revision + per-file
  hashes + approved rewrites). The GitHub workflow
  `package-split-freeze-guard.yml` enforces it on PRs: rules come from the base
  branch and the PR head is inspected, never executed.
- The harness's own tests live in `tests/package_split/` (the `package_split`
  suite, Python 3.9–3.14) and include negative fixtures proving each gate
  fails on a planted violation.

## Rules when touching this domain

- Keep gates fail-closed; never weaken a check, baseline or freeze rule to
  make CI pass. Record skips with reasons instead.
- Never report an unrun gate as passing; evidence paths must be reproducible.
- Schema changes are versioned (`schema_version`); loaders reject unknown
  versions — never silently reinterpret a newer manifest.
- Update `package-contract.toml`, the README and the skill together when
  interfaces change, and extend `tests/package_split/` with negative
  fixtures for any new check.
- Boundaries, names, native replacements and compatibility windows require
  human approval — propose, don't invent.
